  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Freetown Burns Survivor Aftercare project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project was developed to provide clinical skills development to staff at hospitals in Freetown, 
who are managing patients with burns. 
The need for this project was made apparent after the night of 5th &#x2013; 6th November 2021, when there 
was a major explosion following the collision of a fuel tanker and a truck in Freetown, the capital of 
Sierra Leone. People rushed to collect fuel from the leaking vehicle, which then exploded. Over 100 
people died, and 83 survivors were left with ongoing physical and mental health care needs. People 
living with burns injuries need long-term support from a multidisciplinary team who have the 
knowledge and skills to meet their complex needs. In the immediate aftermath of the explosion, it 
was clear that support would be needed for the patients and for the health professionals providing 
care beyond the initial emergency response.</narrative>
  </description>
